Description: Compile AppStream's catalog caches in a short-lived process, not in Discover
 A pool that loads a catalog newer than its cache compiles the cache in the process that loads it,
 and the XML stays in that process's heap for as long as Discover runs.
 Flathub's catalog is 51 MB of XML: about 1,000 MiB resident in Discover against about 200 MiB when the
 pools are filled from compiled caches (measured on an AMD A10-9600P, Debian 6.7.4-3).
 It happens for each user's first launch and again after every change of Flathub's catalog, Discover's
 own pull included, because Debian's apt hook refreshes the system cache of the operating system only
 and a user cannot write the system cache.
 appstreamcli refresh-cache writes the very cache files the pools look for and does nothing when they are
 current, so run it before the places that load Flatpak catalogs: the per-remote pools of the
 Flatpak backend (--datapath), the ratings filter default pool (OdrsReviewsBackend) and the PackageKit
 backend pool.
 If it cannot run, say so in the log and load as before.
 Also say how old the catalog in use is when a refresh fails (offline).
